{
 "result": "sortints.py is built and verified: it reads whitespace-separated integers from stdin and prints them sorted one per line, in about 0.065 s for 1,000,000 ints (about 9x faster than the pure-Python version). It is a thin launcher that execs a compiled C helper, sortints_c (built from sortints_c.c with cc -O2: parse, radix sort, custom itoa, single write), rebuilding it if the source is newer. If there is no compiler or the helper fails to start, it falls back to a numpy version, sortints_np.py. Evidence, on data.txt (1,000,000 random int32 values from gen.py, seed 12345): output is byte-identical to expected.txt, which itself matches `sort -n data.txt`. An edge file (tabs, blank line, +5, 007, negatives, int64 extremes) matches its expected output, empty input gives 0 bytes, and piped input, the numpy fallback and the rebuild path all produce identical output. On `python3 assay.py` (1 / best-of-3 seconds, 0 if output differs) my own runs score 15.0\u201315.5 for the C helper, against 1.67 for pure Python and about 7.1 for the best numpy version. Two caveats: the registered assay baseline is still 1.6668 from the pure-Python version, since I did not re-register it; and at 0.065 s per run the workload is below the 0.3\u20133 s guidance, so Python startup (about 0.03 s) is now roughly half the measured time.",
